<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#telugu-language">Glossary</a> · Term · language</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telugu language</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Telugu is a Dravidian language of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the main language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a> and one of the most widely spoken languages of India. It is written in its own script, derived from Brahmi, and its earliest inscriptions date from the 6th century. Its literature begins with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nannaya">Nannaya</a>'s translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> in the 11th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telugu-language.jpg" alt="Telugu language" width="320" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: C1MM · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Telugu_speaker_map_India.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Nawabs ruled a small enclave surrounded by the Madras Presidency, with a mostly <b>Telugu</b>-speaking Hindu population.</blockquote><a href="/state/banganapalle-state">Banganapalle State</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nawabs ruled a largely Hindu Tamil and <b>Telugu</b> population through local chiefs and revenue farmers.</blockquote><a href="/state/carnatic-sultanate">Carnatic Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Their <b>Telugu</b> inscriptions are among the earliest, and under Rajaraja Narendra the poet Nannaya began the Telugu Mahabharata, the founding work of Telugu literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-chalukyas">Eastern Chalukyas</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qutb Shahs were Shia Muslims who governed a largely <b>Telugu</b>-speaking population and patronised Telugu as well as Persian and Dakhini literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/golconda-sultanate">Golconda Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Jatprole was a hereditary estate held by the Surabhi family, Velama chiefs who had served earlier <b>Telugu</b> and Deccan rulers.</blockquote><a href="/state/jatprole-samasthan">Jatprole Samasthan</a></li><li><blockquote>Ganapatideva united most <b>Telugu</b>-speaking lands and encouraged trade through the port of Motupalli.</blockquote><a href="/state/kakatiya-dynasty">Kakatiya dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
